// DW7 — an image element draws a loading subtree while it is loading and a fallback subtree
// when it failed, and the binding can say which one it drew.
//
// container: it opens a window, so it runs under the container's own X server rather than here.
//
//   ./scripts/gosbox.sh run tests/gui/dw7_image_states.gos
//
// A second run, with a fifo in place, is what makes the *loading* subtree come due — see below.
//
// # What this exists for
//
// Upstream's `image_loading.rs` installs two closures on every cell —
//
//     img(..).with_fallback(|| Self::fallback_element().into_any_element())
//            .with_loading(|| Self::loading_element().into_any_element())
//
// and gpui draws `fallback` for a load that came back `Some(Err)` (`elements/img.rs:366-372`)
// and `loading` while it is `None` and `LOADING_DELAY` (200ms, `:31`) has passed (`:373-395`).
// The binding called neither, so through `ui::image` a still-loading image and an errored one
// were both blank — and nothing in the binding reported a failed image at all.
//
// `gpui::image_request(path, style, loading, fallback)` is the binding's answer: the image
// element keeps the two subtrees as its own, and the binding picks between them by reading the
// ordinary image loader's state, which is the same state `Img` reads. `gpui::image_state(id)`
// reports the decision — `"image"`, `"loading"`, `"fallback"` or `"blank"` — which is the
// failure signal the row was missing.
//
// # What it measures, and what it is not
//
// The claim is that the *right subtree is drawn*, not merely that a flag was set, so the probe
// checks both: `gpui::image_state` says what was chosen, and `gpui::element_bounds` of a marker
// element inside each subtree says whether that subtree was laid out. A flag that lied would
// show up as a state reading with no bounds behind it.
//
// Four cells, all live:
//
//   * `dw7.present` — a file that is there, **with** a fallback subtree. It must read `"image"`,
//     and its fallback marker must never be laid out. This cell is the control for the rule: a
//     fallback subtree that is merely *present* must not be drawn.
//   * `dw7.absent` — a path that is not there, with a fallback subtree. It must read
//     `"fallback"`, and its fallback marker must be laid out.
//   * `dw7.bare` — the same absent path through plain `gpui::image`, no fallback. It must read
//     `"blank"`: there is nothing to draw and nothing was invented.
//   * `dw7.slow` — a fifo, with a loading subtree. See below.
//
// # The loading subtree, and how a load is made to outlast `LOADING_DELAY`
//
// `dw7.slow` points at `/tmp/dw7-slow.pipe`, and the loading subtree is due only while a load
// outlasts 200ms — which no file in this repo does, not even the 4.4MB GIF beside them: the
// loader reads and decodes it well inside a frame, so a run against a real file records the
// loading state as *never due* and says so. Measured, not guessed: with
// `examples/assets/black-cat-typing.gif` in this cell, the loading state is never once recorded.
//
// A fifo is the one source that is not instantaneous and is still just a path: `fs::read` on it
// blocks until a writer arrives (`img.rs:601`), on the background executor (`app.rs:2018`), so
// the load is genuinely pending for as long as the writer keeps it waiting while frames go on.
// The run that witnesses it, and the whole of the harness it needs:
//
//     mkfifo /tmp/dw7-slow.pipe
//     ( sleep 1; cat /work/examples/assets/app-icon.png > /tmp/dw7-slow.pipe ) &
//     gos run --main-thread tests/gui/dw7_image_states.gos
//
// inside the container — `./scripts/gosbox.sh shell` is the same environment. The writer waits
// one second and then hands the loader a *real* PNG, so the load is pending across the 200ms
// mark and then succeeds: the cell reads `"blank"`, then `"loading"`, then `"image"`. That
// writer is also what lets the process exit — a fifo nobody ever writes to leaves the loader
// blocked, and the run would end at the timeout rather than at a verdict.
//
// Without the fifo the cell reads `"blank"` — the path is not there, so the load is a
// `Some(Err)` and this cell has a loading subtree only, nothing to draw — and the probe stays
// green and prints a `note` naming what it did not see, exactly as a probe that cannot witness
// its own subject must. `tests/evidence/DW7.loading.txt` is the transcript of the run that did.
//
// The file the first cell points at is `examples/assets/app-icon.png`, which is this repo's copy
// of upstream's own `examples/image/app-icon.png`; the path that is not there is upstream's
// fourth cell verbatim. Nothing here is a photograph: the container cannot carry a client
// window's pixels into its framebuffer (`tests/evidence/DW3.capture.txt`), so what is checked is
// which subtree the element chose and that it laid out — not what it looks like.

use gpui
use std::process

const PRESENT: &str = "examples/assets/app-icon.png"
const ABSENT: &str = "this/file/really/shouldn't/exist/or/won't/be/an/image/I/hope"
const SLOW: &str = "/tmp/dw7-slow.pipe"

/// A subtree with a name on it, so `gpui::element_bounds` can say whether it was laid out.
///
/// `on_mouse` is what puts an element into the bound map the reader consults — it is the one
/// wrapper the binding records a rectangle for — and the `down` kind is never raised, because
/// nothing drives a pointer here.
fn marker(id: String, colour: String) -> i64 {
    gpui::on_mouse(gpui::rect(f"size=full bg={colour}"), "down", id)
}

fn main() {
    let outcome = gpui::run("dw7 - image states", 520.0, 200.0, || {
        let f = gpui::frame()
        // The loading subtree is an animation upstream, and this probe wants frames whether or
        // not anything is loading — the repaint that brings the loading subtree due arrives at
        // `LOADING_DELAY`, and a program sitting still would otherwise never take it.
        gpui::animate()

        let mut bad = gpui::state_int("bad", 0)
        let mut seen_loading = gpui::state_bool("seen.loading", false)
        let mut load_bounds = gpui::state_float("load.bounds", 0.0)
        let mut first = gpui::state_text("first.state", "")
        let mut transitions = gpui::state_text("transitions", "")

        let root = gpui::row("p=8 gap=8 bg=#111111 size=full")

        // Present file, with both subtrees.
        let present = gpui::image_request(
            *PRESENT,
            "id=dw7.present size=64 border=1 bordercolor=#ff0000",
            marker("dw7.present.load", "#00ff00"),
            marker("dw7.present.fb", "#3333ff"),
        )
        // Absent file, with both subtrees — upstream's cell 4.
        let absent = gpui::image_request(
            *ABSENT,
            "id=dw7.absent size=64 border=1 bordercolor=#ff0000",
            marker("dw7.absent.load", "#00ff00"),
            marker("dw7.absent.fb", "#3333ff"),
        )
        // Absent file through plain `gpui::image`: no fallback, so nothing to draw.
        let bare = gpui::image(*ABSENT, "id=dw7.bare size=64 border=1 bordercolor=#ff0000")
        // A fifo: a load that is pending for as long as its writer keeps it waiting.
        let slow = gpui::image_request(
            *SLOW,
            "id=dw7.slow size=64 border=1 bordercolor=#ff0000",
            marker("dw7.slow.load", "#00ff00"),
            0,
        )

        gpui::child(root, present)
        gpui::child(root, absent)
        gpui::child(root, bare)
        gpui::child(root, slow)
        gpui::root(root)

        // The frame callback is the fourth caller of `gpui::child`, and the render that acts on
        // what it built follows it — so every reading below is one frame behind the tree it is
        // about. Frame 1 is the first render, so frame 2 is the first reading.
        if f < 2 {
            return
        }

        let p = gpui::image_state("dw7.present")
        let a = gpui::image_state("dw7.absent")
        let b = gpui::image_state("dw7.bare")
        let s = gpui::image_state("dw7.slow")
        if first == "" {
            gpui::set_text("first.state", f"{p}/{a}/{b}/{s}")
            first = f"{p}/{a}/{b}/{s}"
        }
        // Every state the slow cell has been seen in, in order: the loading claim is about a
        // *transition*, so the run has to show more than one reading to earn it.
        let last = gpui::state_text("last.state", "")
        if last != s {
            transitions = f"{transitions}{s} "
            gpui::set_text("transitions", transitions)
            gpui::set_text("last.state", s)
        }
        if s == "loading" {
            seen_loading = true
            gpui::set_bool("seen.loading", true)
            // The bounds are read one frame behind the state, so they are taken while the state
            // still says `loading` — taken at the verdict they would be the *next* frame's, by
            // which time the writer may have answered and the subtree be gone.
            let w = gpui::element_bounds("dw7.slow.load", "w")
            if w > 0.0 {
                load_bounds = w
                gpui::set_float("load.bounds", w)
            }
        }

        // The present cell is the one that takes a load: wait for it to settle before judging,
        // and give up after a generous bound so a hang is a verdict rather than a timeout.
        if p != "image" && f < 240 {
            return
        }
        // The slow cell needs its 200ms, and its writer needs its second. With no fifo it stays
        // `blank` for ever, so a bound is what keeps the fifo-less run bounded too. And a load
        // that *did* go through `loading` has to be seen to leave it: a decision cached at
        // `loading` would be a loading subtree stuck on screen for ever, which the state names
        // as one of the three ways this can be wrong.
        let settled = s == "image" || (!seen_loading && f >= 120)
        if !settled && f < 240 {
            return
        }

        if p == "image" {
            println(f"ok     DW7 a file that is there draws the image (state {p}), first reading {first}")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 a file that is there reads {p} and not image (first reading {first})")
            bad = bad + 1
        }
        if a == "fallback" {
            println(f"ok     DW7 a path that is not there draws its fallback subtree (state {a})")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 a path that is not there reads {a} and not fallback")
            bad = bad + 1
        }
        if b == "blank" {
            println(f"ok     DW7 the same path with no fallback subtree reads blank, so nothing is invented (state {b})")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 a path that is not there with no fallback reads {b} and not blank")
            bad = bad + 1
        }

        // The subtrees were laid out, or they were not: this is the half `image_state` cannot
        // speak for.
        let a_fb = gpui::element_bounds("dw7.absent.fb", "w")
        let p_fb = gpui::element_bounds("dw7.present.fb", "w")
        if a_fb > 0.0 {
            println(f"ok     DW7 the fallback subtree was laid out ({a_fb} px wide)")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 the state says fallback but its subtree has no bounds ({a_fb})")
            bad = bad + 1
        }
        if p_fb <= 0.0 {
            println(f"ok     DW7 a fallback subtree that is present but not due was not laid out ({p_fb})")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 the present cell laid out its fallback subtree ({p_fb} px wide) though it drew the image")
            bad = bad + 1
        }

        if seen_loading && load_bounds > 0.0 {
            println(f"ok     DW7 a load that outlasted LOADING_DELAY drew its loading subtree, laid out {load_bounds} px wide (states {transitions})")
            if s == "image" {
                println(f"ok     DW7 and the loading subtree was given up when the load landed (states {transitions})")
            } else {
                println(f"FAIL DW7 the loading subtree is still on screen at {s} though the load should have landed (states {transitions})")
                bad = bad + 1
            }
        } else if seen_loading {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 the slow cell read loading but its subtree has no bounds ({load_bounds})")
            bad = bad + 1
        } else {
            println(f"note   DW7 no load here outlasted LOADING_DELAY, so the loading subtree never came due (states {transitions}) -- measured, not claimed; the run that does is in tests/evidence/DW7.loading.txt")
        }

        if bad == 0 {
            println(f"OK DW7 the image element draws the subtree its load state calls for, and says which one it drew")
        } else {
            println(f"FAIL DW7 {bad} finding(s)")
        }
        gpui::quit()
    })

    if let Err(problem) = outcome {
        eprintln(f"FAIL DW7 gpui::run stopped with an error: {problem}")
        process::exit(1)
    }
}
